// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
; specs/t27f/petal_frontend_viz.t27 -- petal 5 (F FRONTEND) of the interactive visualization epic.
; Refs epic t27#12173 (issue t27#12320; plan row 5: specs/t27f/petal_viz_plan.t27). Built on
; specs/t27f/viz_contract.t27.
; --
; Question: "Which T27F surface answers my intent, and what is its status?"
; Source:   specs/t27f/frontend.t27, the law of T27F's surfaces: the fifteen surfaces (SF_*), their names
;           (SURFACE_NAMES), status (SURFACE_STATUS), maker (SURFACE_MAKER) and MCP tool (surface_tool),
;           the twelve intents (I_*), the route from an intent to the surface it wants (route), the
;           stand-in for a surface that cannot be made this turn (stand_in), the answer (pick), the made
;           claim check (claim_ok) and TRI CLAW's service of a surface (claw_serves, claw_visual_tool).
;           The four hosts (HOST_*) are read from specs/t27f/reel.t27 and the tool rows (TOOLS) from
;           specs/t27mcp/tool_schema.t27, because route takes a host and surface_tool returns a row.
;           Nothing is copied from any of them.
; Truth:    VERIFIED while every shown value is the source's own rule over its own data.
; Reps:     matrix + list + choice + badge = 390 (the plan row; a test holds the two equal).
; --
; What the reader does: reads a matrix of surfaces (rows) by intents (columns) where each cell says
; ANSWERS (the surface is what was wanted and is shown), STAND_IN (shown in place of the wanted one),
; WANTED (wanted but cannot be made, so not shown) or QUIET; reads the same surfaces as a list with
; status, maker, tool and TRI CLAW service; picks an intent on the choice and reads which surface answers
; it, which lights in the matrix and the list; changes the host (the site swaps the graph for its page);
; and may ask a what-if: "suppose surface w could be made this turn".
; --
; Layers of this spec:
;   L1 state       PfvState = the contract's one VizState + the host + the what-if wish. Every contract
;                  event (hover, select, param, focus, back, motion, load) goes through viz_apply. Two
;                  petal events the contract has no word for, PFV_EV_HOST and PFV_EV_WISH, are numbered
;                  above VIZ_EV_COUNT and handled in pfv_apply, which hands everything else to viz_apply.
;                  The badge is a function of the state, so it cannot go stale.
;   L2 entities    surface i = viz_ref(NODE, i); intent k = viz_ref(EDGE, k) (the contract has three
;                  entity kinds, an intent column is the second); cell (i, k) = viz_ref(CELL, i * 16 + k).
;                  Focus order: surfaces 0.., then intents 0..
;   L3 names       the source holds the surface names as one comma joined string and no intent names.
;                  pfv_seg_start and pfv_seg_len read entry k of such a string, so the surface name is
;                  read from SURFACE_NAMES and not copied.
;   L4 answer      pfv_want = route, pfv_shown = pick (the stand-in applied). The wish makes one surface
;                  makeable for the what-if: where an intent wants it, it is shown instead of its stand-in.
;   L5 cell        ANSWERS, STAND_IN, WANTED or QUIET from want and shown, NONE until READY. The tests
;                  hold it to a hand-read table and recompute the answer from route and pick.
;   L6 badge       pfv_badge: UNKNOWN until READY; a truth that cannot claim is kept as it is; otherwise
;                  VERIFIED, and DEMO the moment the input leaves the source's data (the unlisted
;                  "other" intent, or a wish).
;   L7 views       matrix, list, choice and badge each have a layout and an inverse hit test, and all
;                  read the one VizState (row and column highlight from viz_highlight's inputs).
;   L8 host        pfv_host: four root slots; pfv_in_frame embeds them in a larger host (vh_embed).
; --
; Integer only: no floats. Pixel geometry is in host coordinates (the origin is the host's top-left
; corner), so a hit test and pfv_host agree without an offset.
; --
; Recorded petal-side fixtures, nothing else: PFV_INTENT_LABELS (labels only: the source names its
; intents in constants and comments, so it has no name table to read) and the host count (reel.t27 has
; the four HOST_* constants and no count; pfv_hosts is HOST_SITE + 1). The "other" intent, number
; I_COUNT, is the one choice the source does not list; route answers it with SF_NONE, which is the
; source's own word for an unknown intent. Tests carry hand-read oracle tables (PFV_HAND_*).
; Not used from the source, and why: admit() is not called by the source itself (its helper name_ok
; shares a name with tool_schema.t27 name_ok and the splicer refuses an ambiguous name), so the tool
; mount is read through claw_serves. The petal has no stepper: the intents are unordered, so the
; contract's step stays 0 and STEP events change nothing.
; Not claimed: no host draws this petal yet. A plan edit must not import this module's pfv_reps (this
; module imports the plan, so that would be a cycle); the plan holds the number 390 itself.

